<h1>Week 28</h1>

<p><strong>From:</strong> Kayla</p>
<p><strong>Artist:</strong> Brand New</p>
<p><strong>Album:</strong> <em>Your Favorite Weapon</em></p>

<p>Mom,</p>

<p>
My album for you this week is <em>Your Favorite Weapon</em> by Brand New.
</p>

<p>
This is their first album, and I think they are considered part of the big wave of pop punk and modern emo. They would kind of make way for bands like Brand New, Manchester Orchestra, and others down the line.
</p>

<p>
Tamar introduced me to them when we first lived together.
</p>

<p>
It took me a while to adjust to how whiny they sounded, but now I can’t even hear it anymore. But I remember when I first listened, I was like “ew.”
</p>

<p>
My favorites:
</p>

<ul>
<li>Jude Law and a Semester Abroad</li>
<li>Mix Tape</li>
<li>The No Seatbelt Song</li>
<li>Seventy Times 7</li>
<li>Soco Amaretto Lime</li>
</ul>

<p>
“Jude Law and a Semester Abroad” is such a great breakup song.
</p>

<p>
“Mix Tape” is also a really good breakup song.
</p>

<p>
“The No Seatbelt Song” makes me think of Chloe. When we were driving out of Arizona, this song came on and I can vividly remember being right next to her in the car.
</p>

<p>
“Seventy Times 7” is such a good fuck you song, and there is a really interesting story behind it.
</p>

<p>
“Soco Amaretto Lime” always makes me think of Tamar. We used to sing this and play it on guitar all the time.
</p>

<p>
I saved Dad and you from a silly album because I was stuck between two albums, but your choice made me pick this one.
</p>

<p>
Love you,
</p>

<p>
Kayla
</p>

